<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Poetic Edda, Prose Edda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edda is the name of two medieval Icelandic works that preserve Old Norse myth and heroic legend. The Prose Edda, written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a> around 1220, is a handbook of poetics and mythology, while the Poetic Edda is a collection of older anonymous poems known mainly from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of about 1270. Together they are the main sources for Norse mythology.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edda.jpg" alt="Edda" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. G. Collingwood (1854-1932) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Tree_of_Yggdrasil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
